title: Genesis 12 – The Call of Abram
description: A verse-by-verse study of Genesis 12: God calls Abram and makes him promises, Abram calls on the name of the LORD, his lapse of faith in Egypt, and Abraham believed God.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-12-the-call-of-abram.html

# Genesis series pattern (Genesis 1-5). Slide text is the author's own sentences from the Genesis 12 study
# (posts 6757, 6758, 6760, 6761).

=== Introduction
--- Genesis Chapter 12 [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 12: The Call of Abram
sub: The Call • The Promises • The Altar • Egypt
refs: Genesis 12:1-20
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- All of Genesis Was Prologue
kicker: Genesis 12 • The Story of Abram
sub: The Promise of the Man Christ Jesus
p: All of Genesis, as important as the stories of Adam, Cain, Noah, the flood and Babel are, was prologue to this: the story of Abram and Sarai. Abraham and Sarah.
p: Up to this point, mankind who called upon the name of the {Lord} had the promise of His blessing and protection, of God being their Saviour, but not the specific promise of the man Christ Jesus. Born of man. Seed of Abraham. Here we see that the {Lord} appears unto Abram and makes him promises. [[Genesis 4:26]]

=== The call
--- Get Thee Out of Thy Country
kicker: vv. 1–3
sub: I Will Make of Thee a Great Nation
scripture: Genesis 12:1-3 | mark: I will bless them that bless thee, and curse him that curseth thee
p: These are direct blessings and promises to Abram himself. Many try to take the personal promise to Abraham, to bless those that bless him and curse those that curse him, and claim it’s an eternal promise to the modern Jew and the apostate state calling itself Israel. This is found nowhere in scripture. This is God’s promise to protect him and his wife, as God has called Abraham to go to a different land, to Canaan, and there He would establish his legacy.

--- In Thee Shall All Families Be Blessed
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 12:3
fig: Galatians 3:8 | Galatians 3:8 | “And in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed.” The gospel preached before unto Abraham.
fig: Galatians 3:16 | Galatians 3:16 | The specific promise of the man Christ Jesus. Born of man. Seed of Abraham.

--- So Abram Departed
kicker: vv. 4–5
sub: Seventy and Five Years Old
scripture: Genesis 12:4-5 | mark: they went forth to go into the land of Canaan
p: This promise was made to a 75 year old, childless Abram. *And Abram took Sarai his wife, and Lot his brother’s son, and all their substance that they had gathered, and the souls that they had gotten in Haran; and they went forth to go into the land of Canaan.*

--- Unto Thy Seed Will I Give This Land
kicker: vv. 6–8
sub: He Called upon the Name of the {Lord}
scripture: Genesis 12:6-8 | mark: And the Canaanite was then in the land; Unto thy seed will I give this land; called upon the name of the LORD
p: There, God appeared again to him, and gave this more specific promise, in a place where *the Canaanite was then in the land*. His altar built between the Mediterranean and Jordan, between Galilee and Jerusalem.
callout: He was saved by faith through grace. Note the timing of when that was. It comes up later in scripture. [[Romans 4:10]] [[lesson:genesis-17-circumcision|Genesis 17]]

--- The {Lord} Appeared unto Abram
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 12:7
fig: John 1:18 | John 1:18 | The {Lord} appearing to Abraham is considered by many to be an Old Testament appearance of Jesus, a theophany. As the Bible says no man hath seen the Father, many consider the appearance of God unto man in the Old Testament a representation of Christ.
fig: John 1:1-2 | John 1:1-2 | Christ was from the beginning with God, and is God. [[John 8:56-58]]

=== Into Egypt
--- A Famine in the Land
kicker: vv. 9–10
sub: Abram Went Down into Egypt
scripture: Genesis 12:9-10 | mark: went down into Egypt to sojourn there
p: Next we are given a familiar story of a famine in the land, of going to Egypt to sojourn, of Pharaoh and plagues. This theme of finding refuge and sojourning in Egypt is a picture of young Jesus being taken there by Joseph, just as Jesus’ stepfather’s namesake will. [[Matthew 2:13-15]]

--- Say, I Pray Thee, Thou Art My Sister
kicker: vv. 11–13
sub: A Lapse of Faith
scripture: Genesis 12:11-13 | mark: Say, I pray thee, thou art my sister
p: There in Egypt, worried that Sarai, his 65 year old wife, who was still apparently very fair to look upon, would be taken from him, and that he would be killed, Abram tells her to lie and say they are siblings.

--- Taken into Pharaoh’s House
kicker: vv. 14–16
sub: The Woman Was Very Fair
scripture: Genesis 12:14-16

--- The {Lord} Plagued Pharaoh
kicker: vv. 17–20
sub: Take Her, and Go Thy Way
scripture: Genesis 12:17-20 | mark: the LORD plagued Pharaoh and his house with great plagues
p: This seems to be a lack of faith by Abram, and causes enmity with Pharaoh, who indeed was going to seek to betroth Sarai, but now, subject to plagues by the {Lord}, angrily asks them to leave the shelter of his kingdom. God cursed those who would curse Abram, despite his lapse of faith. God made that promise to him, and if he trusted God, he wouldn’t fear for his life. As it stood, God came through anyway, but Abram and all his household were sent away, back to Canaan and the famine.

--- Like Abram, Many of Us Have Doubts
kicker: Application
sub: Faithful unto Death
p: God makes promises to us who believe: to be blessed, to be heirs, to be a blessing to others, to be protected by Him in faith and saved from death, in the imputed righteousness of Jesus. By His grace and mercy. Unfortunately, like Abram, a man of great faith, many of us have doubts. We try to arrange matters and control circumstances out of fear. Sometimes even with convenient lies. Abram, who by faith moved his entire house to a strange land at the behest of God, was never told to go to Egypt in the first place. Let alone to lie to save his own skin.
callout: We should not fear famines or tribulations or even persecution of unregenerate man. We should be faithful unto death, convinced of the hope that we shall be delivered. Even Abram, the father of the seed that contained Christ incarnate, doubted and was weak. But we will see, his faith grew stronger the more he walked with God. [[Revelation 2:10]]

=== Abraham believed God
--- By Faith Abraham Obeyed
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 12:4
fig: Hebrews 11:8 | Hebrews 11:8
fig: James 2:23 | James 2:23 | Abraham believed. This is the verb form of faith, the noun, the thing Abraham had. He believed, having faith in God’s word, His promise. And that faith was imputed unto him for righteousness, and he was called *the Friend of God*. [[2 Chronicles 20:7]]

--- By Faith Abraham Obeyed, continued
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 12:4
fig: Romans 4:3-5 | Romans 4:3-5 | If we believe, an action, if we have faith, the evidence of things unseen, a thing to possess, in the word of God, His promises, His testimony, we have His righteousness imputed on us. Not our own righteousness. Not our works. But our faith, which comes from believing, which comes from the word of God.
callout: O, to be called the friend of God for our faith!

--- Salvation Is by Faith Alone
center: Salvation has always been by hearing the word of God, believing it, which gives us faith to call upon the name of the {Lord}, for which God grants us His grace. Salvation is by faith alone. Yesterday, today and for ever, amen. [[Romans 10:13-17]]
